NAME
    chown - change a file's owner and/or group

SYNOPSIS
    chown <uid|uid:gid|:gid> <file>

DESCRIPTION
    Sets the owning user and/or group of <file>. The owner is NUMERIC - there
    is no /etc/passwd yet, so user and group names are not resolved (that comes
    with the login/permissions arc). Forms:

        uid        set the user, leave the group
        uid:gid    set both
        :gid       set the group, leave the user
        uid:       set the user, leave the group (same as uid)

    ext2 ONLY. Ownership is an ext2 concept; on FAT32, exFAT, or /proc chown
    reports "not supported by this filesystem". ls -l shows the numeric owner
    on ext2 and a dash elsewhere.

EXAMPLES
    chown 0 file          set owner to uid 0 (root)
    chown 501:20 file     set owner 501, group 20
    chown :20 file        set group 20, leave the owner

SEE ALSO
    chmod, ls, stat
